{"tool_name":"seqan","contents":["biocontainers","oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biocontainers":{"name":"seqan","identifiers":["biotools:seqan","doi:10.1186/1471-2105-9-11","doi:10.1016/j.jbiotec.2017.07.017"],"license":"BSD-3-Clause","summary":"SeqAn is an open source C++ library of efficient algorithms and data structures for the analysis of sequences with the focus on biological data.","home_url":"http://www.seqan.de/","keywords":["Databases, Genetic","Genome, Human","Genomics","High-Throughput Nucleotide Sequencing","Humans","Sequence Alignment","Sequence Analysis, DNA","Comparative genomics","Metagenomics","Transcriptomics","Bioinformatics","Software engineering"],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":0},"biotools":{"id":"seqan","home":"https://www.seqan.de","license":"BSD-3-Clause","summary":"An open source C++ library of efficient algorithms and data structures for the analysis of sequences with the focus on biological data. Our library applies a unique generic design that guarantees high performance, generality, extensibility, and integration with other libraries. The library is easy to use and simplifies the development of new software tools with a minimal loss of performance. Additionally, it includes several ready-made command line tools for common tasks in sequence analysis.","addition_date":"2016-01-19T16:16:51Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T20:16:30.079413Z","tool_type":["Workbench"],"version":["2.4.0","3.1.0"],"collections":["KNIME","SeqAn","de.NBI"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:seqan","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"seqan-dev@lists.fu-berlin.de","name":"Mailing list answered by the developers","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]},{"email":"seqan-dev@lists.fu-berlin.de","name":"Mailing list answered by the developers","typeRole":["Developer"]},{"name":"Center for Integrative Bioinformatics (CiBi)","typeEntity":"Consortium"},{"name":"Freie Universit\u00e4t Berlin","typeEntity":"Institute","typeRole":["Provider"]},{"name":"cibi","typeEntity":"Institute","typeRole":["Provider"]}],"documentation":[{"note":"SeqAn 3.1.0 || API SeqAn 2.4.0","type":["API documentation","General"],"url":"https://docs.seqan.de"},{"note":"Tutorials SeqAn 2.4.0","type":["General"],"url":"https://seqan.readthedocs.org/en/master/index.html"},{"type":["Citation instructions"],"url":"https://www.seqan.de"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["eseiler"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"input":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2044"},"format":[{"term":"EMBL-like (text)","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2181"},{"term":"FASTA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1929"},{"term":"FASTQ-like format (text)","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2182"},{"term":"GenBank-like format (text)","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2205"}]},{"data":{"term":"Sequence alignment","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_0863"},"format":[{"term":"BAM","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2572"},{"term":"SAM","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2573"}]}],"operation":[{"term":"Nucleic acid sequence analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2478"},{"term":"Read mapping","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3198"},{"term":"Sequence alignment","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0292"},{"term":"Sequence analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2403"},{"term":"Variant calling","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3227"}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"SeqAn","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"rrahn","publication":[{"doi":"10.1186/1471-2105-9-11","metadata":{"abstract":"Background: The use of novel algorithmic techniques is pivotal to many important problems in life science. For example the sequencing of the human genome 1 would not have been possible without advanced assembly algorithms. However, owing to the high speed of technological progress and the urgent need for bioinformatics tools, there is a widening gap between state-of-the-art algorithmic techniques and the actual algorithmic components of tools that are in widespread use. Results: To remedy this trend we propose the use of SeqAn, a library of efficient data types and algorithms for sequence analysis in computational biology. SeqAn comprises implementations of existing, practical state-of-the-art algorithmic components to provide a sound basis for algorithm testing and development. In this paper we describe the design and content of SeqAn and demonstrate its use by giving two examples. In the first example we show an application of SeqAn as an experimental platform by comparing different exact string matching algorithms. The second example is a simple version of the well-known MUMmer tool rewritten in SeqAn. Results indicate that our implementation is very efficient and versatile to use. Conclusion: We anticipate that SeqAn greatly simplifies the rapid development of new bioinformatics tools by providing a collection of readily usable, well-designed algorithmic components which are fundamental for the field of sequence analysis. This leverages not only the implementation of new algorithms, but also enables a sound analysis and comparison of existing algorithms. \u00a9 2008 D\u00f6ring et al; licensee BioMed Central Ltd.","authors":[{"name":"Doring A."},{"name":"Rausch T."},{"name":"Reinert K."},{"name":"Weese D."}],"citationCount":225,"date":"2008-01-09T00:00:00Z","journal":"BMC Bioinformatics","title":"SeqAn an efficient, generic C++ library for sequence analysis"},"pmcid":"PMC2246154","pmid":"18184432","type":["Primary"]},{"doi":"10.1016/j.jbiotec.2017.07.017","metadata":{"abstract":"Background The use of novel algorithmic techniques is pivotal to many important problems in life science. For example the sequencing of the human genome (Venter et al., 2001) would not have been possible without advanced assembly algorithms and the development of practical BWT based read mappers have been instrumental for NGS analysis. However, owing to the high speed of technological progress and the urgent need for bioinformatics tools, there was a widening gap between state-of-the-art algorithmic techniques and the actual algorithmic components of tools that are in widespread use. We previously addressed this by introducing the SeqAn library of efficient data types and algorithms in 2008 (D\u00f6ring et al., 2008). Results The SeqAn library has matured considerably since its first publication 9 years ago. In this article we review its status as an established resource for programmers in the field of sequence analysis and its contributions to many analysis tools. Conclusions We anticipate that SeqAn will continue to be a valuable resource, especially since it started to actively support various hardware acceleration techniques in a systematic manner.","authors":[{"name":"Dadi T.H."},{"name":"Ehrhardt M."},{"name":"Hauswedell H."},{"name":"Kim J."},{"name":"Mehringer S."},{"name":"Pockrandt C."},{"name":"Rahn R."},{"name":"Reinert K."},{"name":"Siragusa E."},{"name":"Urgese G."},{"name":"Weese D."},{"name":"Winkler J."}],"citationCount":63,"date":"2017-11-10T00:00:00Z","journal":"Journal of Biotechnology","title":"The SeqAn C++ template library for efficient sequence analysis: A resource for programmers"},"pmid":"28888961","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Bioinformatics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0091"},{"term":"Comparative genomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0797"},{"term":"Metagenomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3174"},{"term":"Software engineering","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3372"},{"term":"Transcriptomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3308"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"SeqAn","home":"https://bio.tools/seqan","license":"BSD-3-Clause","version":["2.4.0","3.1.0"],"summary":"An open source C++ library of efficient algorithms and data structures for the analysis of sequences with the focus on biological data. Our library applies a unique generic design that guarantees high performance, generality, extensibility, and integration with other libraries. The library is easy to use and simplifies the development of new software tools with a minimal loss of performance. Additionally, it includes several ready-made command line tools for common tasks in sequence analysis.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:seqan:2.0.1/workbench/www.seqan.de","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"binaries":{"freeness":true,"registration":false},"packages":true,"source_packages":true,"sourcecode":{"free":true,"interpreted":false,"license":true,"public":true,"readme":true,"repository":{"anonymous":true,"online":true}}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"citation":true,"general":true},"license":{"open_source":true,"osi":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":122,"citations":[{"count":2,"year":"2026"},{"count":3,"year":"2010"},{"count":3,"year":"2013"},{"count":3,"year":"2020"},{"count":3,"year":"2022"},{"count":3,"year":"2023"},{"count":4,"year":"2021"},{"count":4,"year":"2025"},{"count":6,"year":"2024"},{"count":7,"year":"2018"},{"count":8,"year":"2011"},{"count":8,"year":"2012"},{"count":8,"year":"2014"},{"count":8,"year":"2016"},{"count":10,"year":"2009"},{"count":12,"year":"2015"},{"count":14,"year":"2019"},{"count":16,"year":"2017"}],"doi":"10.1186/1471-2105-9-11","pmcid":"PMC2246154","pmid":"18184432","ref_count":17,"refs":[{"count":1,"year":"1970"},{"count":1,"year":"1982"},{"count":1,"year":"1987"},{"count":1,"year":"1990"},{"count":1,"year":"1996"},{"count":1,"year":"2001"},{"count":1,"year":"2005"},{"count":1,"year":"2006"},{"count":2,"year":"2000"},{"count":2,"year":"2003"},{"count":2,"year":"2004"},{"count":3,"year":"2002"}],"title":"SeqAn an efficient, generic C++ library for sequence analysis.","year":2008}]}],"readme":true,"summary":{"d